A||||21||fiero56||slow starting||||1||
Z||000000||fiero56||02-15-2000||10:04 AM||||I've got another problem/question. I've been in this mood all week, anything that isn't perfect really gets on my nerves.<BR>I have an '88 Formula(v-6 5-speed). Beginning yesterday the Fiero doesn't start up immediatly like it used to. Usually on the cold start in the morning, I wouldn't even get a chance to hear the starter because the engine would start so quick. But lately in the morning it takes a good second or so of cranking before the engine starts. And it doesn't fire strongly right away. It takes a moment for the idle to shoot up to 1,500. Temps are the same they have been for a while. <BR>I haven't checked fuel pressure yet(no guage), once started the engine runs/performs great. No changes there. Gas level is full. It did throw its very first code at me last week. Coolant Temp Sensor. I resest the codes and it hasn't come back though. Is it a possibilty. Whats going on here.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-15-2000||11:14 AM||||I had an 88 2.8 beretta, a mechanic did a scan and found the temp sender reading minus forty with the vehicle inside the shop.  Do Fieros have the same sensor setups and would this cause hard start?||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||02-15-2000||02:43 PM||||I believe the Beretta would use a similar if not identical coolant temp setup. If the ECM thinks it's -40 when it's not that could definitely affect the starting. The amount of fuel injected at startup is varied based on coolant temp.<P>The Coolant Temp Sensor sounds like a likely suspect. The sensor can be bad and not set a code. The code is only set if the sensor reading is way too high or low. The ECM has no way of knowing if the calibration is right. As always, check the wiring for bad connections also.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||02-15-2000||03:26 PM||||My Fiero V6 did what it sounds like yours does.  It cranked a few extra cranks and when it did fire sounded like fouled plugs.  After 2 weeks of this, the coil pooped out when I was on vacation in Fargo ND.  No problems after that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ITA_FIERO||02-15-2000||10:26 PM||||My 86 SE (6 cyl, 5 speed) did the same thing for a while.  It was the fuel pump relay that had gone bad.  The following quote is from the Haynes manual and was the one that got me going in the right direction. "An inoperative fuel pump relay can result in long cranking times, particularly if the engine is cold."  Once I figured out what the heck it was, it was easy to replace and no more hard starts.  The relay switches on the fuel pump for a couple of seconds upon activating the starter so fuel gets to the engine immediately.  If the relay is bad, you have to crank it a couple of times to get oil pressure (4+PSI) which would then kick on the fuel pump also.  If it's not that, I have no guesses.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||02-15-2000||10:46 PM||||I had also thought of the relay, but before cranking, when I put the ignition in "on" I can hear the fuel pump pressurizing. Correct me if I'm wrong, but If the fuel pump pressurizes, doesn't that mean the relay is good?<P><BR>Another question. While cheking for any stored codes(there were none)in diagnostic mode, my IAC valve was rapidly clicking. It stopped clicking when I unplugged it(duh, just checking to make sure your still awake), any only clicks in diagnostic mode with the ignition in the "on" position. When out of diagnostic mode, it did not click. I could feel the pintle(?) clicking? It was mechanical as I could feel it through the IAC valve housing, and could hear it through my "screwdriver stethoscope". Could this mean anything? <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ITA_FIERO||02-15-2000||11:19 PM||||I agree with you that it isn't the relay if you can hear the pump when you cut on the ignition.<BR>Interesting thought with the IAC valve.  It does provide the function of adding additional air flow during cold starts!  If the valve is bad (or the wiring to it) that could be your problem.  I wonder if the reason you can't hear it in NON-diagnostic mode is because until you turn on the ignition (engage the starter) there is no signal from the ECM to the IAC valve?  That might at least explain hearing it in diag mode and not otherwise?!  Then it boils down to testing the wiring versus testing the IAC valve.<BR>PS I've never heard mine "clicking," but then I've never really paid attention during diag mode.  Is it loud enough you would notice if you weren't listening?||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTBro||02-16-2000||12:25 AM||||I heard mine doing that today.  I was checking for trouble codes and checking to see that I had proper vacuum pressure and I heard the ticking noise you are talking about while in diagnostic mode.<P>I had no idea what it was.  I only heard it when I was out of the car and looking into the engine compartment.  I could not hear it when I was sitting in the car.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||02-16-2000||08:36 AM||||I could hear it while sitting in the car with the window down. But I was in a garage(and the decklid was open). At first I thought it was the flouresent(sp?) lights above, but tracked it down to the IAC. <BR>I do hear a clicking when the engine is running but always assumed it was the fuel system. I'll check once I start 'er up this morning.<BR>The wiring looks good, but I'll do a good check on it. Might just replace the valve and see what happens. My idle isn't rock solid when the engine is warmed up. Not all the time, but occasionally the idle will vary about 50rpm back and forth.<BR>Is it normal for the IAC to make noise while the ECM is in dagnostic mode?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||02-16-2000||09:10 PM||||I've been giving my delayed starting some more thought. Is it possible the cold start injector isn't doing its job, or not very well. If the car is warmed up and been driven a while, and I park it, say for 5 minutes, it fires right up, but not in the morning. I know the cold start injector "activates" if coolant temp is below 95F. Just a thought.  <BR>Also, this "problem" has been going on for a few weeks now. When I do a cold start, it fires up, and does the high idle at 1700-1800 rpms(instead of the regular 1500) and drops down from there. I know it isn't the tach's fault because the engine does sound like it is idling higher than normal. What would cause this higher cold idle? These things won't get out of my head.<P><BR>Thinking here. Is it possible the coolant sensor is sending the ECM the wrong resistance, telling it to run richer than it should cold(higher cold rpm), and somehow making it more difficult to start the engine in the morning? I have no idea where this sensor is, but if possible, would like to hook up my meter, and see what the given resistance is. Any takers on my theory, or anyone have any other ideas?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||02-17-2000||10:25 AM||||Anyone? I'm posting relplies to my own posts.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||02-17-2000||01:54 PM||||Fiero56, <P>Whatever the problem is mine (also 88 Formula V6 5spd) must have caught it!!! <P>The last few times I started it cold it has been doing exactly as you described (no SE light or codes though). It seems to start a little quicker if I push down on the accelerator a little (also eliminates the rough running before going to 1500+ RPM).<P>I think the IAC noise in diagnostic mode is normal.<P>It may be this weekend before I have time to look at mine. I'll post if I find anything. I'm thinking of checking the temp circuit and cold start circuit. The weak coil that wkayl mentioned sounds like a possibility also.<P>This link give the resistance values of the ECM coolant sensor. It is located under the EGR solenoid on the front of the engine and has a black and a yellow wire running to it. <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/coolant-sensor.html</A> <P>The cold start circuit is independent of the ECM. Here's a link with some info. <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/cold-start-injector.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~fierosketch/osg/cold-start-injector.html</A> <P>Good luck, David.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||fiero56||02-17-2000||02:05 PM||||Hey DRH,<BR>great information, I wasn't looking forward to digging around the 'net looking for resistance values. I'll try and get a meter on the temp circuit, and if time permits the cold start circuit too. It hasn't thrown any more codes, I think the one it gave me was false, maybe a little air in the line or something, it never came back. I was just in there for several hours today, digging around just looking, following wires, vaccume lines, etc. Nothing visible, I'm wondering(hoping) it has to do with the coolant temp sensor that runs to the ECM. Is the engine ever supposed to have cold idle higher than 1500? Like maybe if the sensor said it was -40F(and it was +20F), the engine would run a little higher like mine does? Maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000013||ITA_FIERO||02-17-2000||09:35 PM||||I've sure been thinking about it but I don't have much for you.  The IAC valve is certainly a possibility (or did you eliminate it?) as is the cold start valve (or injector).  Several ways to test the cold start valve with the easiest being to disconnect it from the fuel rail, put it in a jar, turn the car over once(when cold), and if fuel comes out then obviously the injector works.  Even then, could be a clog in the line within the fuel rail beyond the injector but that's not as likely.  You can also test the current through the injector as well.  My bet is that it is either the IAC or cold start valve.  Coolant sensor shouldn't affect starting.||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||02-17-2000||10:00 PM||||The temp sensor dose the same thing for fuel injection that a choke dose for a carburetor. Hence the symptoms will be similar, enough said.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||02-18-2000||08:14 AM||||88,<BR>So your saying it is possible the coolant sensor could be to blame? Awsome. I don't have time to check today, but I'll be on it Saturday morning.<P>Question, mainly directed at Ogre, but anyone is free to answer. While rummaging through my Haynes, it warned not to take resistance readings directly off the ECM since it is solid state. I'm not going to damage anything if I take these readings off the coolant sensor while it is still connected am I?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Phil||02-18-2000||09:42 AM||||I'm not an electronics guru but I believe the reason for not taking readings off the computer is that your ohm meter puts a voltage into the circuit being tested. This voltage although small could be harmful to the computer. Someone please correct me if I'm wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DRH||02-18-2000||01:45 PM||||Fiero56,<P>Phil is correct about why you don't measure resistance of the ECM. I don't know if it would be enough to hurt anything or not but it would be inaccurate anyway. The ECM circuit will have some amount of resistence which will throw the resistance reading of the sensor off. You need to unplug the sensor to measure it's resistance.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000018||theogre||02-18-2000||04:18 PM||||The sensors are easy to check with an ECM scanner. You can read each one the ECM uses as the ECM sees it. So if the air is 0F and the coolant on a cold morning start is reading 90, then there's a problem.<P>Yes, Avoid putting an Ohm meter across any active circuit or across contacts on the ECM. The least you get is a useless reading. You could damage the device, in this case the ECM, depending on just what contacts you measure across.<P>try just checking to make sure all the connectors are clean and tight. Dirty or loose contact will change the reading across the sender.<P>I'd replace the ECM's coolant sender anyway if it's the original one. You can get them on Wrenchead. I don't know what that one costs. Get the Borg-Warner or AC-Delco ones.<P>Besides that cars are just plane harder to start cold even when everything works perfectly. I'm not exactly sure what your problems is. Are you turning the key to start several times before it starts, or just one long crank?<P>If the problem is only happening below freezing temps, try some dry gas in the next couple fill ups. You could have some water in the tank or filter. The drygas usually pulls it out as long as it's only a little bit. You should not need to add drygas every tank.<P>If you can remove the cold start injector without a big pain you might want to soak the tip of it with a little cleaner. Don't soak the whole thing in cleaner or you could wreck it. <P>Not all V6's have the cold start injector. Some time in 86 or 87 I think GM deleted it. They just run the rest with bigger pulses when cold.||||reg||1
Z||000019||ITA_FIERO||02-18-2000||06:40 PM||||Just for me to know, if the IAC provides extra air flow during cold start, and the Cold Start Injector adds additional fuel during cold start, what exactly does the coolant temp sensor do during starts?  I was thinking it advances timing some and adds some additional fuel while the engine is coming to temp, but I was unaware that it directly influenced starting. I also thought the cold start injector operated regardless of sensor input (had it's own metallic switch that "flipped" at roughly 95 degrees)<BR>I have no written basis for my thoughts but I can't find anything contrary to them either, so now I'm confused...again...okay, I'm always confused but worse than normal now.<p>[This message has been edited by ITA_FIERO (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||otis||02-18-2000||08:22 PM||||I just rebuilt my 86GT and noticed hard<BR>starting when cold. Today I checked the<BR>shrader valve port with a fuel pressure<BR>gauge. The reading went to 47 psi which<BR>is correct, but did not hold. Next step <BR>is to pinch off the return line and again<BR>check pressure. If if fails to hold then<BR>pressurize and pinch feed line and look<BR>for stable pressure. If you still lose<BR>pressure, then the  injectors or the cold<BR>start valve are leaking down. In order to<BR>check this without a fuel gauge you must<BR>pull the upper plenum and pull the injectors<BR>up and watch for a leaking tip upon activation of the  pump. Quick tip, is<BR>you don't have a gauge and good indicator<BR>of a fuel system problem is to take a rag<BR>and a  small  screwdriver and with the engine<BR>cold or after stiting several hours. Depress<BR>the shrader valve  on the fuel rail next to<BR>the fuel lines. If you have little or no<BR>pressure at this point, you fuel line is<BR>not staying pressurized and this tells you<BR>to toubleshoot the fuel system rather than<BR>ECM or sensors to begin locating the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000021||fiero56||02-18-2000||09:58 PM||||I think I'll just buy a new coolant sensor. I bought one for another vehicle(not Fiero) today and installed it to fix a random stall conditon. Wow! What a difference. I don't know what all it changed but cold starts are quick, and solid. Idle is perfect and smooth at all temps, works perfect. And for that particular car it was only $13(borg-warner too). Think I'll try and get one for the Fiero soon. If it doesn't solve the problem, I'll be posting again.<BR>I don't have to do multiple cranks, but recently has started taking at least one second for the engine to finally start when cold. Warm starts(within 10 minutes) are the normal fast, and the 1-2 hour starts take the normal 3-4 seconds.||||reg||1
